{{define "berth" -}}
## Deploying with Berth

This app runs on [Berth](https://github.com/dps/berth), a small PaaS on a Mac.
Each app runs in its own Linux micro-VM behind a proxy. The `berth` commands
below work on that Mac, and from any machine that reaches it over SSH: there,
the CLI runs them on the Mac, which it finds from this repo's `berth` git
remote. If the remote is missing, ask the user to run
`BERTH_HOST=<user>@<mac> berth git:remote {{.App}}` in the repo.

- **Production**: {{.Prod}}. Every push to `{{.Branch}}` on GitHub deploys it.
  Only push to `{{.Branch}}` when the user asks you to ship.
- **Dev version**: {{.Dev}}, for trying changes before they're committed.
  `berth dev:push` deploys the working tree as it is on disk, uncommitted
  changes included. Add `--json` for a structured result. Exit code 0 means
  live, 1 the build failed, 2 the app failed to start, 3 the release command
  (migrations) failed; the output says why. A failed push leaves the previous
  dev release serving.
- The dev version isn't public. Requests need a token:
  `curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $BERTH_DEV_TOKEN" {{.Dev}}/`.
  If `BERTH_DEV_TOKEN` isn't set, ask the user to run
  `berth dev:token {{.App}} --as test@example.com`; requests are then signed
  in as that test user.

### What the app must do

1. Listen on `$PORT` (8080 unless set) on `0.0.0.0`.
2. Read configuration and secrets from environment variables. Never commit
   secrets: set them with `berth config:set {{.App}} KEY=value` (the app
   restarts) and `berth config:set {{.App}}-dev KEY=value` for the dev version.
3. Write files only to `/tmp`, a mounted folder
   (`berth storage:mount {{.App}} /uploads`) or the SQLite database (below);
   everything else is reset on each deploy.
4. Log to stdout and stderr.

A deploy switches traffic once `/` answers with a status below 500 (set
`BERTH_HEALTHCHECK=/healthz` to check another path). Berth detects the stack
and builds it; a `Dockerfile` at the repo root replaces that, and
`berth dockerfile {{.App}}` prints the one Berth generates.

### Background work

- **Workers**: a line in the `Procfile` other than `web` and `release`, such
  as `worker: node worker.js`, runs as another process inside the app's
  container (same build, config and storage), restarted if it exits. It shares
  the app's memory. A web process is still required. `berth ps:scale
  {{.App}} worker=2` runs more; each sees its name in `BERTH_PROCESS`.
- **Scheduled jobs**: a `"cron"` list in `app.json`, for example
  `{"cron": [{"command": "node digest.js", "schedule": "0 7 * * *"}]}`, in the
  Mac's local time. Each run is a fresh container; a run still going when the
  next is due is skipped. Dev versions and previews don't run schedules: try a
  job with `berth cron:run {{.App}}-dev 1`, see them with `berth cron {{.App}}`
  and their output with `berth logs {{.App}} --cron`.
- Pull requests may get previews, `{{.App}}-pr-<number>`, if the user has
  turned them on (`berth previews {{.App}}`): a check on the PR links to it.

### Looking at it

- `berth logs {{.App}}-dev -n 200` (and `{{.App}}` for production); `--build`
  for the last build, `--release` for the last release command, `-f` to follow.
- `berth status {{.App}}`, `berth releases {{.App}}`, `berth config:show {{.App}}`.
- One-off commands in the app's environment: `berth run {{.App}}-dev <command>`.

### Data

- Postgres: `berth pg:create {{.App}}-db && berth pg:link {{.App}}-db {{.App}}`
  sets `DATABASE_URL`. Redis: `berth redis:create` and `redis:link` set
  `REDIS_URL`. The dev version gets its own empty database, never production's;
  `berth dev:db:copy {{.App}} -y` loads a copy of production's data.
- SQLite: `berth sqlite:add {{.App}}` sets `DATABASE_URL=sqlite:////data/sqlite/app.db`
  and `SQLITE_PATH=/data/sqlite/app.db`; open that file, never one in a mounted
  folder. The app then runs in one container at a time: deploys stop the old
  version first, and scheduled jobs run inside the app, like its workers, so
  both can open the database. `berth sqlite:shell {{.App}}-dev` opens it. The dev version has its own.
- Schema changes are migrations, run by a `release:` line in a `Procfile`
  (for example `release: npm run migrate`) on every deploy, before the new
  code takes traffic. While a deploy swaps over, the old code runs against the
  new schema: add columns before using them, and drop them only a release
  after the code stops using them. Try migrations with `berth dev:push` first.
{{- if .SignIn}}

### Sign-in

Berth runs Google sign-in for the app, so don't write OAuth code. It works
only once the app has an **access policy**, and a new app has none:
`berth access {{.App}}` shows it. Without one, the headers below never arrive
and the `/_berth/` links answer 404 in production.

- `berth access {{.App}} public`: anyone can visit; signed-in people are
  identified. Use this for "Sign in" links on an open site.
- `berth access {{.App}} signed-in`: any Google account, or
  `berth access {{.App}} allow @owners` for a group.
- Per path: `berth access {{.App}} /admin/* allow @owners`.

Setting the policy changes production: ask the user to run it, and do that
before shipping code that relies on sign-in. The dev version always has a
policy (`@owners`), so sign-in working there doesn't mean it works in
production.

With a policy, requests carry `X-Berth-User-Id` (stable; key users on it),
`X-Berth-Email`, `X-Berth-Name` (URL-encoded) and `X-Berth-Picture`. Berth
strips these from visitors, so they can be trusted. On a `public` app they're
absent for anonymous visitors: check them on write endpoints and return 401
without them. Berth serves these paths on the app's own host:
`/_berth/login?return=/path` (sign in, then come back), a POST form to
`/_berth/logout?return=/path`, and `/_berth/me` (JSON) for single-page apps.

To test on the dev version, a token made with `--as` signs requests in as
that user; one made without `--as` makes them anonymous.
{{- end}}
{{- if .AI}}

### AI

`berth ai:enable {{.App}}` gives the app an LLM through Berth's gateway: it sets
`OPENAI_BASE_URL`, `OPENAI_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` and
`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, which the official OpenAI and Anthropic SDKs read on their
own. Ask for the model `default`, or see `berth ai:models`.
{{- end}}

### Ask the user first

Pushing to `{{.Branch}}`, `berth rollback`, `berth destroy`, changing
production's config or access policy, and `berth dev:db:copy` all affect real users or data:
ask before running them. Never push to `{{.Branch}}` just to test something;
that's what `berth dev:push` is for.
{{- end}}
